 implemented, so we don't need to modify it significantly. It's listed first as it's the primary reference implementation.\n\n2. `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py:_get_response` and `_check_classifier_response_method` are part of the visualization infrastructure that will need to be used or extended to support the new plotting function. The base classes handle the common plotting logic and validation for metrics plots.\n\n3. `sklearn/calibration.py:CalibrationDisplay` is the main plotting class that will handle the visualization of calibration curves. It already exists with similar functionality for ROC curves, and we can extend it similarly for the new calibration curve plot.\n\n4. The order of arguments in the proposed function (plot_calibration_curve) is intentional, as it matches the current calibration_curve function's return values (prop_true, prop_pred), which makes it more intuitive for users familiar with the calibration curve concept.\n\nThis implementation would align with the existing calibration plotting framework in scikit-learn, maintaining consistency in both functionality and user interface design.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "Verify if the found locations contain all the necessary information to address the issue, and check for any relevant references in other parts of the codebase that may not have appeared in the search results. If not, continue searching for additional locations related to the issue.\nVerify that you have carefully analyzed the impact of the found locations on the repository, especially their dependencies. If you think you have solved the task, rewrite your final answer into the required location-only format, output ONLY the location block, and then call `finish` to finish.\nIMPORTANT: YOU SHOULD NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\n"}, {"content": "Let me verify the completeness of the found locations and check for additional relevant code:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>