function is _beta_divergence(X, WH) and is minimized with an\n742 |     alternating minimization of W and H. Each minimization is done with a\n743 |     Multiplicative Update.\n744 | \n745 |     Parameters\n746 |     ----------\n747 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n748 |         Constant input matrix.\n749 | \n750 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n751 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n752 | \n753 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n754 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n755 | \n756 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n757 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n758 |         String must be in {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', 'itakura-saito'}.\n759 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n760 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n761 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n762 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n763 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros.\n764 | \n765 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n766 |         Number of iterations.\n767 | \n768 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n769 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n770 | \n771 |     l1_reg_W : float, default=0.\n772 |         L1 regularization parameter for W.\n773 | \n774 |     l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n775 |         L1 regularization parameter for H.\n776 | \n777 |     l2_reg_W : float, default=0.\n778 |         L2 regularization parameter for W.\n779 | \n780 |     l2_reg_H : float, default=0.\n781 |         L2 regularization parameter for H.\n782 | \n783 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n784 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n785 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n786 | \n787 |     verbose : int, default=0\n788 |         The verbosity level.\n789 | \n790 |     Returns\n791 |     -------\n792 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n793 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n794 | \n795 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n796 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n797 | \n798 |     n_iter : int\n799 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n800 | \n801 |     References\n802 |     ----------\n803 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n804 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n805 |     \"\"\"\n806 |     start_time = time.time()\n807 | \n808 |     beta_loss = _beta_loss_to_float(beta_loss)\n809 | \n810 |     # gamma for Maximization-Minimization (MM) algorithm [Fevotte 2011]\n811 |     if beta_loss < 1:\n812 |         gamma = 1.0 / (2.0 - beta_loss)\n813 |     elif beta_loss > 2:\n814 |         gamma = 1.0 / (beta_loss - 1.0)\n815 |     else:\n816 |         gamma = 1.0\n817 | \n818 |     # used for the convergence criterion\n819 |     error_at_init = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n820 |     previous_error = error_at_init\n821 | \n822 |     H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n823 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n824 |         # update W\n825 |         # H_sum, HHt and XHt are saved and reused if not update_H\n826 |         delta_W, H_sum, HHt, XHt = _multiplicative_update_w(\n827 |             X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, gamma, H_sum, HHt, XHt, update_H\n828 |         )\n829 |         W *= delta_W\n830 | \n831 |         # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n832 |         if beta_loss < 1:\n833 |             W[W < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n834 | \n835 |         # update H\n836 |         if update_H:\n837 |             delta_H = _multiplicative_update_h(\n838 |                 X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma\n839 |             )\n840 |             H *= delta_H\n841 | \n842 |             # These values will be recomputed since H changed\n843 |             H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n844 | \n845 |             # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n846 |             if beta_loss <= 1:\n847 |                 H[H < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n848 | \n849 |         # test convergence criterion every 10 iterations\n850 |         if tol > 0 and n_iter % 10 == 0:\n851 |             error = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n852 | \n853 |             if verbose:\n854 |                 iter_time = time.time()\n855 |                 print(\n856 |                     \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds, error: %f\"\n857 |                     % (n_iter, iter_time - start_time, error)\n858 |                 )\n859 | \n860 |             if (previous_error - error) / error_at_init < tol:\n861 |                 break\n862 |             previous_error = error\n863 | \n864 |     # do not print if we have already printed in the convergence test\n865 |     if verbose and (tol == 0 or n_iter % 10 != 0):\n866 |         end_time = time.time()\n867 |         print(\n868 |             \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds.\" % (n_iter, end_time - start_time)\n869 |         )\n870 | \n871 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSRegression(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that 3 PLS packages\n    # provided in the R language (R-project):\n    #     - \"mixOmics\" with function pls(X, Y, mode = \"regression\")\n    #     - \"plspm \" with function plsreg2(X, Y)\n    #     - \"pls\" with function oscorespls.fit(X, Y)\n\n    def __init__(\n        self, n_components=2, *, scale=True, max_iter=500, tol=1e-06, copy=True\n    ):\n...\n    def fit(self, X, Y):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSCanonical(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that the \"plspm\" package\n    # provided in the R language (R-project), using the function plsca(X, Y).\n    # Results are equal or collinear with the function\n    # ``pls(..., mode = \"canonical\")`` of the \"mixOmics\" package. The\n    # difference relies in the fact that mixOmics implementation does not\n    # exactly implement the Wold algorithm since it does not normalize\n    # y_weights to one.\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        n_components=2,\n        *,\n        scale=True,\n        algorithm=\"nipals\",\n        max_iter=500,\n        tol=1e-06,\n        copy=True,\n    ):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_multiplicative_update_h`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n643 | def _multiplicative_update_h(X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma):\n644 |     \"\"\"Update H in Multiplicative Update NMF.\"\"\"\n645 |     if beta_loss == 2:\n646 |         numerator = safe_sparse_dot(W.T, X)\n647 |         denominator = np.linalg.multi_dot([W.T, W, H])\n648 | \n649 |     else:\n650 |         # Numerator\n651 |         WH_safe_X = _special_sparse_dot(W, H, X)\n652 |         if sp.issparse(X):\n653 |             WH_safe_X_data = WH_safe_X.data\n654 |             X_data = X.data\n655 |         else:\n656 |             WH_safe_X_data = WH_safe_X\n657 |             X_data = X\n658 |             # copy used in the Denominator\n659 |             WH = WH_safe_X.copy()\n660 |             if beta_loss - 1.0 < 0:\n661 |                 WH[WH == 0] = EPSILON\n662 | \n663 |         # to avoid division by zero\n664 |         if beta_loss - 2.0 < 0:\n665 |             WH_safe_X_data[WH_safe_X_data == 0] = EPSILON\n666 | \n667 |         if beta_loss == 1:\n668 |             np.divide(X_data, WH_safe_X_data, out=WH_safe_X_data)\n669 |         elif beta_loss == 0:\n670 |             # speeds up computation time\n671 |             # refer to /numpy/numpy/issues/9363\n672 |             WH_safe_X_data **= -1\n673 |             WH_safe_X_data **= 2\n674 |             # element-wise multiplication\n675 |             WH_safe_X_data *= X_data\n676 |         else:\n677 |             WH_safe_X_data **= beta_loss - 2\n678 |             # element-wise multiplication\n679 |             WH_safe_X_data *= X_data\n680 | \n681 |         # here numerator = dot(W.T, (dot(W, H) ** (beta_loss - 2)) * X)\n682 |         numerator = safe_sparse_dot(W.T, WH_safe_X)\n683 | \n684 |         # Denominator\n685 |         if beta_loss == 1:\n686 |             W_sum = np.sum(W, axis=0)  # shape(n_components, )\n687 |             W_sum[W_sum == 0] = 1.0\n688 |             denominator = W_sum[:, np.newaxis]\n689 | \n690 |         # beta_loss not in (1, 2)\n691 |         else:\n692 |             # computation of WtWH = dot(W.T, dot(W, H) ** beta_loss - 1)\n693 |             if sp.issparse(X):\n694 |                 # memory efficient computation\n695 |                 # (compute column by column, avoiding the dense matrix WH)\n696 |                 WtWH = np.empty(H.shape)\n697 |                 for i in range(X.shape[1]):\n698 |                     WHi = np.dot(W, H[:, i])\n699 |                     if beta_loss - 1 < 0:\n700 |                         WHi[WHi == 0] = EPSILON\n701 |                     WHi **= beta_loss - 1\n702 |                     WtWH[:, i] = np.dot(W.T, WHi)\n703 |             else:\n704 |                 WH **= beta_loss - 1\n705 |                 WtWH = np.dot(W.T, WH)\n706 |             denominator = WtWH\n707 | \n708 |     # Add 