ameter=search_terms>['sklearn/datasets/_base.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"sklearn/datasets/_base.py\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/datasets/_base.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_base.py`.\n```\n   1 | \"\"\"\n   2 | Base IO code for all datasets\n   3 | \"\"\"\n   4 | \n   5 | # Copyright (c) 2007 David Cournapeau <cournape@gmail.com>\n   6 | #               2010 Fabian Pedregosa <fabian.pedregosa@inria.fr>\n   7 | #               2010 Olivier Grisel <olivier.grisel@ensta.org>\n   8 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n   9 | import os\n  10 | import csv\n  11 | import shutil\n  12 | from collections import namedtuple\n  13 | from os import environ, listdir, makedirs\n  14 | from os.path import dirname, exists, expanduser, isdir, join, splitext\n  15 | import hashlib\n  16 | \n  17 | from ..utils import Bunch\n  18 | from ..utils import check_random_state\n  19 | from ..utils import check_pandas_support\n  20 | from ..utils.validation import _deprecate_positional_args\n  21 | \n  22 | import numpy as np\n  23 | \n  24 | from urllib.request import urlretrieve\n  25 | \n  26 | RemoteFileMetadata = namedtuple('RemoteFileMetadata',\n  27 |                                 ['filename', 'url', 'checksum'])\n  28 | \n  29 | \n  30 | def get_data_home(data_home=None) -> str:\n  31 |     \"\"\"Return the path of the scikit-learn data dir.\n  32 | \n  33 |     This folder is used by some large dataset loaders to avoid downloading the\n  34 |     data several times.\n  35 | \n  36 |     By default the data dir is set to a folder named 'scikit_learn_data' in the\n  37 |     user home folder.\n  38 | \n  39 |     Alternatively, it can be set by the 'SCIKIT_LEARN_DATA' environment\n  40 |     variable or programmatically by giving an explicit folder path. The '~'\n  41 |     symbol is expanded to the user home folder.\n  42 | \n  43 |     If the folder does not already exist, it is automatically created.\n  44 | \n  45 |     Parameters\n  46 |     ----------\n  47 |     data_home : str | None\n  48 |         The path to scikit-learn data dir.\n  49 |     \"\"\"\n  50 |     if data_home is None:\n  51 |         data_home = environ.get('SCIKIT_LEARN_DATA',\n  52 |                                 join('~', 'scikit_learn_data'))\n  53 |     data_home = expanduser(data_home)\n  54 |     if not exists(data_home):\n  55 |         makedirs(data_home)\n  56 |     return data_home\n  57 | \n  58 | \n  59 | def clear_data_home(data_home=None):\n  60 |     \"\"\"Delete all the content of the data home cache.\n  61 | \n  62 |     Parameters\n  63 |     ----------\n  64 |     data_home : str | None\n  65 |         The path to scikit-learn data dir.\n  66 |     \"\"\"\n  67 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home)\n  68 |     shutil.rmtree(data_home)\n  69 | \n  70 | \n  71 | def _convert_data_dataframe(caller_name, data, target,\n  72 |                             feature_names, target_names):\n  73 |     pd = check_pandas_support('{} with as_frame=True'.format(caller_name))\n  74 |     data_df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns=feature_names)\n  75 |     target_df = pd.DataFrame(target, columns=target_names)\n  76 |     combined_df = pd.concat([data_df, target_df], axis=1)\n  77 |     X = combined_df[feature_names]\n  78 |     y = combined_df[target_names]\n  79 |     if y.shape[1] == 1:\n  80 |         y = y.iloc[:, 0]\n  81 |     return combined_df, X, y\n  82 | \n  83 | \n  84 | @_deprecate_positional_args\n  85 | def load_files(container_path, *, description=None, categories=None,\n  86 |                load_content=True, shuffle=True, encoding=None,\n  87 |                decode_error='strict', random_state=0):\n  88 |     \"\"\"Load text files with categories as subfolder names.\n  89 | \n  90 |     Individual samples are assumed to be files stored a two levels folder\n  91 |     structure such as the following:\n  92 | \n  93 |         container_folder/\n  94 |             category_1_folder/\n  95 |                 file_1.txt\n  96 |                 file_2.txt\n  97 |                 ...\n  98 |                 file_42.txt\n  99 |             category_2_folder/\n 100 |                 file_43.txt\n 101 |                 file_44.txt\n 102 |                 ...\n 103 | \n 104 |     The folder names are used as supervised signal label names. The individual\n 105 |     file names are not important.\n 106 | \n 107 |     This function does not try to extract features into a numpy array or scipy\n 108 |     sparse matrix. In addition, if load_content is false it does not try to\n 109 |     load the files in memory.\n 110 | \n 111 |     To use text files in a scikit-learn classification or clustering algorithm,\n 112 |     you will need to use the :mod`~sklearn.feature_extraction.text` module to\n 113 |     build a feature extraction transformer that suits your problem.\n 114 | \n 115 |     If you set load_content=True, you should also specify the encoding of the\n 116 |     text using the 'encoding' parameter. For many modern text files, 'utf-8'\n 117 |     will be the correct encoding. If you leave encoding equal to None, then the\n 118 |     content will be made of bytes instead of Unicode, and you will not be able\n 119 |     to use most functions in :mod:`~sklearn.feature_extraction.text`.\n 120 | \n 121 |     Similar feature extractors should be built for other kind of unstructured\n 122 |     data input such as images, audio, video, ...\n 123 | \n 124 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <datasets>`.\n 125 | \n 126 |     Parameters\n 127 |     ----------\n 128 |     container_path : str or unicode\n 129 |         Path to the main folder holding one subfolder per category\n 130 | \n 131 |     description : str or unicode, default=None\n 132 |         A paragraph describing the characteristic of the dataset: its source,\n 133 |         reference, etc.\n 134 | \n 135 |     categories : list of str, default=None\n 136 |         If None (default), load all the categories. If not None, list of\n 137 |         category names to load (other categories ignored).\n 138 | \n 139 |     load_content : bool, default=True\n 140 |         Whether to load or not the content of the different files. If true a\n 141 |         'data' attribute containing the text information is present in the data\n 142 |         structure returned. If not, a filenames attribute gives the path to the\n 143 |         files.\n 144 | \n 145 |     shuffle : bool, default=True\n 146 |         Whether or not to shuffle the data: might be important for models that\n 147 |         make the assumption that the samples are independent and identically\n 148 |         distributed (i.i.d.), such as stochastic gradient descent.\n 149 | \n 150 |     encoding : str, default=None\n 151 |         If None, do not try to decode the content of the files (e.g. for images\n 152 |         or other non-text content). If not None, encoding to use to decode text\n 153 |         files to Unicode if load_content is True.\n 154 | \n 155 |     decode_error : {'strict', 'ignore', 'replace'}, default='strict'\n 156 |         Instruction on what to do if a byte sequence is given to analyze that\n 157 |         contains characters not of the given `encoding`. Passed as keyword\n 158 |         argument 'errors' to bytes.decode.\n 159 | \n 160 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=0\n 161 |         Determines random number generation for dataset shuffling. Pass an int\n 162 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n 163 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n 164 | \n 165 |     Returns\n 166 |     -------\n 167 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 168 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n 169 | \n 170 |         data : list of str\n 171 |             Only present when `load_content=True`.\n 172 |             The raw text data to learn.\n 173 |         target : ndarray\n 174 |             The target labels (integer index).\n 175 |         target_names : list\n 176 |             The names of target classes.\n 177 |         DESCR : str\n 178 |             The full description of the dataset.\n 179 |         filenames: ndarray\n 180 |             The filenames holding the dataset.\n 181 |     \"\"\"\n 182 |     target = []\n 183 |     target_names = []\n 184 |     filenames = []\n 185 | \n 186 |     folders = [f for f in sorted(listdir(container_path))\n 187 |                if isdir(join(container_path, f))]\n 188 | \n 189 |     if categories is not None:\n 190 |         folders = [f for f in folders if f in categories]\n 191 | \n 192 |     for label, folder in enumerate(folders):\n 193 |         target_names.append(folder)\n 194 |         folder_path = join(container_path, folder)\n 195 |         documents = [join(folder_path, d)\n 196 |                      for d in sorted(listdir(folder_path))]\n 197 |         target.extend(len(documents) * [label])\n 198 |         filenames.extend(documents)\n 199 | \n 200 |     # convert to array for fancy indexing\n 201 |     filenames = np.array(filenames)\n 202 |     target = np.array(target)\n 203 | \n 204 |     if shuffle:\n 205 |         random_state = check_random_state(random_state)\n 206 |         indices = np.arange(filenames.shape[0])\n 207 |         random_state.shuffle(indices)\n 208 |         filenames = filenames[indices]\n 209 |         target = target[indices]\n 210 | \n 211 |     if load_content:\n 212 |         data = []\n 213 |         for filename in filenames:\n 214 |             with open(filename, 'rb') as f:\n 215 |                 data.append(f.read())\n 216 |         if encoding is not None:\n 217 |             data = [d.decode(encoding, decode_error) for d in data]\n 218 |         return Bunch(data=data,\n 219 |                      filenames=filenames,\n 220 |                      target_names=target_names,\n 221 |                      target=target,\n 222 |                      DESCR=description)\n 223 | \n 224 |     return Bunch(filenames=filenames,\n 225 |                  target_names=target_names,\n 226 |                  target=target,\n 227 |                  DESCR=description)\n 228 | \n 229 | \n 230 | def load_data(module_path, data_file_name):\n 231 |     \"\"\"Loads data from module_path/data/data_file_name.\n 232 | \n 233 |     Parameters\n 234 |     ----------\n 235 |     module_path : string\n 236 |         The module path.\n 237 | \n 238 |     data_file_name : string\n 239 |         Name of csv file to be loaded from\n 240 |         module_path/data/data_file_name. For example 'wine_data.csv'.\n 241 | \n 242 |     Returns\n 243 |     -------\n 244 |     data : Numpy array\n 245 |         A 2D array with each row representing one sample and each column\n 246 |         representing the features of a given sample.\n 247 | \n 248 |     target : Numpy array\n 249 |         A 1D array holding target variables for all the samples in `data.\n 250 |         For example target[0] is the target varible for data[0].\n 251 | \n 252 |     target_names : Numpy array\n 253 |         A 1D array containing the names of the classifications. For example\n 254 |         target_names[0] is the name of the target[0] class.\n 255 |     \"\"\"\n 256 |     with open(join(module_path, 'data', data_file_name)) as csv_file:\n 257 |         data_file = csv.reader(csv_file)\n 258 |         temp = next(data_file)\n 259 |         n_samples = int(temp[0])\n 260 |         n_features = int(temp[1])\n 261 |         target_names = np.array(temp[2:])\n 262 |         data = np.empty((n_samples, n_features))\n 263 |         target = np.empty((n_samples,), dtype=int)\n 264 | \n 265 |         for i, ir in enumerate(data_file):\n 266 |             data[i] = np.asarray(ir[:-1], dtype=np.float64)\n 267 |             target[i] = np.asarray(ir[-1], dtype=int)\n 268 | \n 269 |     return data, target, target_names\n 270 | \n 271 | \n 272 | @_deprecate_positional_args\n 273 | def load_wine(*, return_X_y=False, as_frame=False):\n 274 |     \"\"\"Load and return the wine dataset (classification).\n 275 | \n 276 |     .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 277 | \n 278 |     The wine dataset is a classic and very easy multi-class classification\n 279 |     dataset.\n 280 | \n 281 |     =================   ==============\n 282 |     Classes                          3\n 283 |     Samples per class        [59,71,48]\n 284 |     Samples total                  178\n 285 |     Dimensionality                  13\n 286 |     Features            real, positive\n 287 |     =================   ==============\n 288 | \n 289 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <wine_dataset>`.\n 290 | \n 291 |     Parameters\n 292 |     ----------\n 293 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n 294 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object.\n 295 |         See below for more information about the `data` and `target` object.\n 296 | \n 297 |     as_frame : bool, default=False\n 298 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n 299 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric). The target is\n 300 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target columns.\n 301 |         If `return_X_y` is True, then (`data`, `target`) will be pandas\n 302 |         DataFrames or Series as described below.\n 303 | \n 304 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 305 | \n 306 |     Returns\n 307 |     -------\n 308 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 309 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n 310 | \n 311 |         data : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (178, 13)\n 312 |             The data matrix. If `as_frame=True`, `data` will be a pandas\n 313 |             DataFrame.\n 314 |         target: {ndarray, Series} of shape (178,)\n 315 |             The classification target. If `as_frame=True`, `target` will be\n 316 |             a pandas Series.\n 317 |         feature_names: list\n 318 |             The names of the dataset columns.\n 319 |         target_names: list\n 320 |             The names of target classes.\n 321 |         frame: DataFrame of shape (178, 14)\n 322 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with `data` and\n 323 |             `target`.\n 324 | \n 325 |             .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 326 |         DESCR: str\n 327 |             The full description of the dataset.\n 328 | \n 329 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n 330 | \n 331 |     The copy of UCI ML Wine Data Set dataset is downloaded and modified to fit\n 332 |     standard format from:\n 333 |     https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/machine-learning-databases/wine/wine.data\n 334 | \n 335 |     Examples\n 336 |     --------\n 337 |     Let's say you are interested in the samples 10, 80, and 140, and want to\n 338 |     know their class name.\n 339 | \n 340 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import load_wine\n 341 |     >>> data = load_wine()\n 342 |     >>> data.target[[10, 80, 140]]\n 343 |     array([0, 1, 2])\n 344 |     >>> list(data.target_names)\n 345 |     ['class_0', 'class_1', 'class_2']\n 346 |     \"\"\"\n 347 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n 348 |     data, target, target_names = load_data(module_path, 'wine_data.csv')\n 349 | \n 350 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'wine_data.rst')) as rst_file:\n 351 |         fdescr = rst_file.read()\n 352 | \n 353 |     feature_names = ['alcohol',\n 354 |                      'malic_acid',\n 355 |                      'ash',\n 356 |                      'alcalinity_of_ash',\n 357 |                      'magnesium',\n 358 |                      'total_phenols',\n 359 |                      'flavanoids',\n 360 |                      'nonflavanoid_phenols',\n 361 |                      'proanthocyanins',\n 362 |                      'color_intensity',\n 363 |                      'hue',\n 364 |                      'od280/od315_of_diluted_wines',\n 365 |                      'proline']\n 366 | \n 367 |     frame = None\n 368 |     target_columns = ['target', ]\n 369 |     if as_frame:\n 370 |         frame, data, target = _convert_data_dataframe(\"load_wine\",\n 371 |                                                       data,\n 372 |                                                       target,\n 373 |                                                       feature_names,\n 374 |                                                       target_columns)\n 375 | \n 376 |     if return_X_y:\n 377 |         return data, target\n 378 | \n 379 |     return Bunch(data=data,\n 380 |                  target=target,\n 381 |                  frame=frame,\n 382 |                  target_names=target_names,\n 383 |                  DESCR=fdescr,\n 384 |                  feature_names=feature_names)\n 385 | \n 386 | \n 387 | @_deprecate_positional_args\n 388 | def load_iris(*, return_X_y=False, as_frame=False):\n 389 |     \"\"\"Load and return the iris dataset (classification).\n 390 | \n 391 |     The iris dataset is a classic and very easy multi-class classification\n 392 |     dataset.\n 393 | \n 394 |     =================   ==============\n 395 |     Classes                          3\n 396 |     Samples per class               50\n 397 |     Samples total                  150\n 398 |     Dimensionality                   4\n 399 |     Features            real, positive\n 400 |     =================   ==============\n 401 | \n 402 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <iris_dataset>`.\n 403 | \n 404 |     Parameters\n 405 |     ----------\n 406 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n 407 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n 408 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` object.\n 409 | \n 410 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 411 | \n 412 |     as_frame : bool, default=False\n 413 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n 414 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric). The target is\n 415 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target columns.\n 416 |         If `return_X_y` is True, then (`data`, `target`) will be pandas\n 417 |         DataFrames or Series as described below.\n 418 | \n 419 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 420 | \n 421 |     Returns\n 422 |     -------\n 423 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 424 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n 425 | \n 426 |         data : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (150, 4)\n 427 |             The data matrix. If `as_frame=True`, `data` will be a pandas\n 428 |             DataFrame.\n 429 |         target: {ndarray, Series} of shape (150,)\n 430 |             The classification target. If `as_frame=True`, `target` will be\n 431 |             a pandas Series.\n 432 |         feature_names: list\n 433 |             The names of the dataset columns.\n 434 |         target_names: list\n 435 |             The names of target classes.\n 436 |         frame: DataFrame of shape (150, 5)\n 437 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with `data` and\n 438 |             `target`.\n 439 | \n 440 |             .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 441 |         DESCR: str\n 442 |             The full description of the dataset.\n 443 |         filename: str\n 444 |             The path to the location of the data.\n 445 | \n 446 |             .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 447 | \n 448 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n 449 | \n 450 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 451 | \n 452 |     Notes\n 453 |     -----\n 454 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.20\n 455 |             Fixed two wrong data points according to Fisher's paper.\n 456 |             The new version is the same as in R, but not as in the UCI\n 457 |             Machine Learning Repository.\n 458 | \n 459 |     Examples\n 460 |     --------\n 461 |     Let's say you are interested in the samples 10, 25, and 50, and want to\n 462 |     know their class name.\n 463 | \n 464 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\n 465 |     >>> data = load_iris()\n 466 |     >>> data.target[[10, 25, 50]]\n 467 |     array([0, 0, 1])\n 468 |     >>> list(data.target_names)\n 469 |     ['setosa', 'versicolor', 'virginica']\n 470 |     \"\"\"\n 471 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n 472 |     data, target, target_names = load_data(module_path, 'iris.csv')\n 473 |     iris_csv_filename = join(module_path, 'data', 'iris.csv')\n 474 | \n 475 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'iris.rst')) as rst_file:\n 476 |         fdescr = rst_file.read()\n 477 | \n 478 |     feature_names = ['sepal length (cm)', 'sepal width (cm)',\n 479 |                      'petal length (cm)', 'petal width (cm)']\n 480 | \n 481 |     frame = None\n 482 |     target_columns = ['target', ]\n 483 |     if as_frame:\n 484 |         frame, data, target = _convert_data_dataframe(\"load_iris\",\n 485 |                                                       data,\n 486 |                                                       target,\n 487 |                                                       feature_names,\n 488 |                                                       target_columns)\n 489 | \n 490 |     if return_X_y:\n 491 |         return data, target\n 492 | \n 493 |     return Bunch(data=data,\n 494 |                  target=target,\n 495 |                  frame=frame,\n 496 |                  target_names=target_names,\n 497 |                  DESCR=fdescr,\n 498 |                  feature_names=feature_names,\n 499 |                  filename=iris_csv_filename)\n 500 | \n 501 | \n 502 | @_deprecate_positional_args\n 503 | def load_breast_cancer(*, return_X_y=False, as_frame=False):\n 504 |     \"\"\"Load and return the breast cancer wisconsin dataset (classification).\n 505 | \n 506 |     The breast cancer dataset is a classic and very easy binary classification\n 507 |     dataset.\n 508 | \n 509 |     =================   ==============\n 510 |     Classes                          2\n 511 |     Samples per class    212(M),357(B)\n 512 |     Samples total                  569\n 513 |     Dimensionality                  30\n 514 |     Features            real, positive\n 515 |     =================   ==============\n 516 | \n 517 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <breast_cancer_dataset>`.\n 518 | \n 519 |     Parameters\n 520 |     ----------\n 521 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n 522 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object.\n 523 |         See below for more information about the `data` and `target` object.\n 524 | \n 525 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 526 | \n 527 |     as_frame : bool, default=False\n 528 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n 529 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric). The target is\n 530 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target columns.\n 531 |         If `return_X_y` is True, then (`data`, `target`) will be pandas\n 532 |         DataFrames or Series as described below.\n 533 | \n 534 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 535 | \n 536 |     Returns\n 537 |     -------\n 538 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 539 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n 540 | \n 541 |         data : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (569, 30)\n 542 |             The data matrix. If `as_frame=True`, `data` will be a pandas\n 543 |             DataFrame.\n 544 |         target: {ndarray, Series} of shape (569,)\n 545 |             The classification target. If `as_frame=True`, `target` will be\n 546 |             a pandas Series.\n 547 |         feature_names: list\n 548 |             The names of the dataset columns.\n 549 |         target_names: list\n 550 |             The names of target classes.\n 551 |         frame: DataFrame of shape (569, 31)\n 552 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with `data` and\n 553 |             `target`.\n 554 | \n 555 |             .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 556 |         DESCR: str\n 557 |             The full description of the dataset.\n 558 |         filename: str\n 559 |             The path to the location of the data.\n 560 | \n 561 |             .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 562 | \n 563 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n 564 | \n 565 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 566 | \n 567 |     The copy of UCI ML Breast Cancer Wisconsin (Diagnostic) dataset is\n 568 |     downloaded from:\n 569 |     https://goo.gl/U2Uwz2\n 570 | \n 571 |     Examples\n 572 |     --------\n 573 |     Let's say you are interested in the samples 10, 50, and 85, and want to\n 574 |     know their class name.\n 575 | \n 576 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancer\n 577 |     >>> data = load_breast_cancer()\n 578 |     >>> data.target[[10, 50, 85]]\n 579 |     array([0, 1, 0])\n 580 |     >>> list(data.target_names)\n 581 |     ['malignant', 'benign']\n 582 |     \"\"\"\n 583 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n 584 |     data, target, target_names = load_data(module_path, 'breast_cancer.csv')\n 585 |     csv_filename = join(module_path, 'data', 'breast_cancer.csv')\n 586 | \n 587 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'breast_cancer.rst')) as rst_file:\n 588 |         fdescr = rst_file.read()\n 589 | \n 590 |     feature_names = np.array(['mean radius', 'mean texture',\n 591 |                               'mean perimeter', 'mean area',\n 592 |                               'mean smoothness', 'mean compactness',\n 593 |                               'mean concavity', 'mean concave points',\n 594 |                               'mean symmetry', 'mean fractal dimension',\n 595 |                               'radius error', 'texture error',\n 596 |                               'perimeter error', 'area error',\n 597 |                               'smoothness error', 'compactness error',\n 598 |                               'concavity error', 'concave points error',\n 599 |                               'symmetry error', 'fractal dimension error',\n 600 |                               'worst radius', 'worst texture',\n 601 |                               'worst perimeter', 'worst area',\n 602 |                               'worst smoothness', 'worst compactness',\n 603 |                               'worst concavity', 'worst concave points',\n 604 |                               'worst symmetry', 'worst fractal dimension'])\n 605 | \n 606 |     frame = None\n 607 |     target_columns = ['target', ]\n 608 |     if as_frame:\n 609 |         frame, data, target = _convert_data_dataframe(\"load_breast_cancer\",\n 610 |                                                       data,\n 611 |                                                       target,\n 612 |                                                       feature_names,\n 613 |                                                       target_columns)\n 614 | \n 615 |     if return_X_y:\n 616 |         return data, target\n 617 | \n 618 |     return Bunch(data=data,\n 619 |                  target=target,\n 620 |                  frame=frame,\n 621 |                  target_names=target_names,\n 622 |                  DESCR=fdescr,\n 623 |                  feature_names=feature_names,\n 624 |                  filename=csv_filename)\n 625 | \n 626 | \n 627 | @_deprecate_positional_args\n 628 | def load_digits(*, n_class=10, return_X_y=False, as_frame=False):\n 629 |     \"\"\"Load and return the digits dataset (classification).\n 630 | \n 631 |     Each datapoint is a 8x8 image of a digit.\n 632 | \n 633 |     =================   ==============\n 634 |     Classes                         10\n 635 |     Samples per class             ~180\n 636 |     Samples total                 1797\n 637 |     Dimensionality                  64\n 638 |     Features             integers 0-16\n 639 |     =================   ==============\n 640 | \n 641 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <digits_dataset>`.\n 642 | \n 643 |     Parameters\n 644 |     ----------\n 645 |     n_class : int, default=10\n 646 |         The number of classes to return. Between 0 and 10.\n 647 | \n 648 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n 649 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object.\n 650 |         See below for more information about the `data` and `target` object.\n 651 | \n 652 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 653 | \n 654 |     as_frame : bool, default=False\n 655 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n 656 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric). The target is\n 657 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target columns.\n 658 |         If `return_X_y` is True, then (`data`, `target`) will be pandas\n 659 |         DataFrames or Series as described below.\n 660 | \n 661 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 662 | \n 663 |     Returns\n 664 |     -------\n 665 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 666 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n 667 | \n 668 |         data : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (1797, 64)\n 669 |             The flattened data matrix. If `as_frame=True`, `data` will be\n 670 |             a pandas DataFrame.\n 671 |         target: {ndarray, Series} of shape (1797,)\n 672 |             The classification target. If `as_frame=True`, `target` will be\n 673 |             a pandas Series.\n 674 |         feature_names: list\n 675 |             The names of the dataset columns.\n 676 |         target_names: list\n 677 |             The names of target classes.\n 678 | \n 679 |             .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 680 | \n 681 |         frame: DataFrame of shape (1797, 65)\n 682 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with `data` and\n 683 |             `target`.\n 684 | \n 685 |             .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 686 |         images: {ndarray} of shape (1797, 8, 8)\n 687 |             The raw image data.\n 688 |         DESCR: str\n 689 |             The full description of the dataset.\n 690 | \n 691 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n 692 | \n 693 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 694 | \n 695 |     This is a copy of the test set of the UCI ML hand-written digits datasets\n 696 |     https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Optical+Recognition+of+Handwritten+Digits\n 697 | \n 698 |     Examples\n 699 |     --------\n 700 |     To load the data and visualize the images::\n 701 | \n 702 |         >>> from sklearn.datasets import load_digits\n 703 |         >>> digits = load_digits()\n 704 |         >>> print(digits.data.shape)\n 705 |         (1797, 64)\n 706 |         >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt #doctest: +SKIP\n 707 |         >>> plt.gray() #doctest: +SKIP\n 708 |         >>> plt.matshow(digits.images[0]) #doctest: +SKIP\n 709 |         >>> plt.show() #doctest: +SKIP\n 710 |     \"\"\"\n 711 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n 712 |     data = np.loadtxt(join(module_path, 'data', 'digits.csv.gz'),\n 713 |                       delimiter=',')\n 714 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'digits.rst')) as f:\n 715 |         descr = f.read()\n 716 |     target = data[:, -1].astype(int, copy=False)\n 717 |     flat_data = data[:, :-1]\n 718 |     images = flat_data.view()\n 719 |     images.shape = (-1, 8, 8)\n 720 | \n 721 |     if n_class < 10:\n 722 |         idx = target < n_class\n 723 |         flat_data, target = flat_data[idx], target[idx]\n 724 |         images = images[idx]\n 725 | \n 726 |     feature_names = ['pixel_{}_{}'.format(row_idx, col_idx)\n 727 |                      for row_idx in range(8)\n 728 |                      for col_idx in range(8)]\n 729 | \n 730 |     frame = None\n 731 |     target_columns = ['target', ]\n 732 |     if as_frame:\n 733 |         frame, flat_data, target = _convert_data_dataframe(\"load_digits\",\n 734 |                                                            flat_data,\n 735 |                                                            target,\n 736 |                                                            feature_names,\n 737 |                                                            target_columns)\n 738 | \n 739 |     if return_X_y:\n 740 |         return flat_data, target\n 741 | \n 742 |     return Bunch(data=flat_data,\n 743 |                  target=target,\n 744 |                  frame=frame,\n 745 |                  feature_names=feature_names,\n 746 |                  target_names=np.arange(10),\n 747 |                  images=images,\n 748 |                  DESCR=descr)\n 749 | \n 750 | \n 751 | @_deprecate_positional_args\n 752 | def load_diabetes(*, return_X_y=False, as_frame=False):\n 753 |     \"\"\"Load and return the diabetes dataset (regression).\n 754 | \n 755 |     ==============   ==================\n 756 |     Samples total    442\n 757 |     Dimensionality   10\n 758 |     Features         real, -.2 < x < .2\n 759 |     Targets          integer 25 - 346\n 760 |     ==============   ==================\n 761 | \n 762 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <diabetes_dataset>`.\n 763 | \n 764 |     Parameters\n 765 |     ----------\n 766 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False.\n 767 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object.\n 768 |         See below for more information about the `data` and `target` object.\n 769 | \n 770 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 771 | \n 772 |     as_frame : bool, default=False\n 773 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n 774 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric). The target is\n 775 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target columns.\n 776 |         If `return_X_y` is True, then (`data`, `target`) will be pandas\n 777 |         DataFrames or Series as described below.\n 778 | \n 779 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 780 | \n 781 |     Returns\n 782 |     -------\n 783 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 784 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n 785 | \n 786 |         data : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (442, 10)\n 787 |             The data matrix. If `as_frame=True`, `data` will be a pandas\n 788 |             DataFrame.\n 789 |         target: {ndarray, Series} of shape (442,)\n 790 |             The regression target. If `as_frame=True`, `target` will be\n 791 |             a pandas Series.\n 792 |         feature_names: list\n 793 |             The names of the dataset columns.\n 794 |         frame: DataFrame of shape (442, 11)\n 795 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with `data` and\n 796 |             `target`.\n 797 | \n 798 |             .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 799 |         DESCR: str\n 800 |             The full description of the dataset.\n 801 |         data_filename: str\n 802 |             The path to the location of the data.\n 803 |         target_filename: str\n 804 |             The path to the location of the target.\n 805 | \n 806 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n 807 | \n 808 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 809 |     \"\"\"\n 810 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n 811 |     base_dir = join(module_path, 'data')\n 812 |     data_filename = join(base_dir, 'diabetes_data.csv.gz')\n 813 |     data = np.loadtxt(data_filename)\n 814 |     target_filename = join(base_dir, 'diabetes_target.csv.gz')\n 815 |     target = np.loadtxt(target_filename)\n 816 | \n 817 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'diabetes.rst')) as rst_file:\n 818 |         fdescr = rst_file.read()\n 819 | \n 820 |     feature_names = ['age', 'sex', 'bmi', 'bp',\n 821 |                      's1', 's2', 's3', 's4', 's5', 's6']\n 822 | \n 823 |     frame = None\n 824 |     target_columns = ['target', ]\n 825 |     if as_frame:\n 826 |         frame, data, target = _convert_data_dataframe(\"load_diabetes\",\n 827 |                                                       data,\n 828 |                                                       target,\n 829 |                                                       feature_names,\n 830 |                                                       target_columns)\n 831 | \n 832 |     if return_X_y:\n 833 |         return data, target\n 834 | \n 835 |     return Bunch(data=data,\n 836 |                  target=target,\n 837 |                  frame=frame,\n 838 |                  DESCR=fdescr,\n 839 |                  feature_names=feature_names,\n 840 |                  data_filename=data_filename,\n 841 |                  target_filename=target_filename)\n 842 | \n 843 | \n 844 | @_deprecate_positional_args\n 845 | def load_linnerud(*, return_X_y=False, as_frame=False):\n 846 |     \"\"\"Load and return the physical excercise linnerud dataset.\n 847 | \n 848 |     This dataset is suitable for multi-ouput regression tasks.\n 849 | \n 850 |     ==============   ============================\n 851 |     Samples total    20\n 852 |     Dimensionality   3 (for both data and target)\n 853 |     Features         integer\n 854 |     Targets          integer\n 855 |     ==============   ============================\n 856 | \n 857 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <linnerrud_dataset>`.\n 858 | \n 859 |     Parameters\n 860 |     ----------\n 861 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n 862 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object.\n 863 |         See below for more information about the `data` and `target` object.\n 864 | \n 865 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 866 | \n 867 |     as_frame : bool, default=False\n 868 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n 869 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n 870 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target columns.\n 871 |         If `return_X_y` is True, then (`data`, `target`) will be pandas\n 872 |         DataFrames or Series as described below.\n 873 | \n 874 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 875 | \n 876 |     Returns\n 877 |     -------\n 878 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 879 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n 880 | \n 881 |         data : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (20, 3)\n 882 |             The data matrix. If `as_frame=True`, `data` will be a pandas\n 883 |             DataFrame.\n 884 |         target: {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (20, 3)\n 885 |             The regression targets. If `as_frame=True`, `target` will be\n 886 |             a pandas DataFrame.\n 887 |         feature_names: list\n 888 |             The names of the dataset columns.\n 889 |         target_names: list\n 890 |             The names of the target columns.\n 891 |         frame: DataFrame of shape (20, 6)\n 892 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with `data` and\n 893 |             `target`.\n 894 | \n 895 |             .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 896 |         DESCR: str\n 897 |             The full description of the dataset.\n 898 |         data_filename: str\n 899 |             The path to the location of the data.\n 900 |         target_filename: str\n 901 |             The path to the location of the target.\n 902 | \n 903 |             .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 904 | \n 905 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n 906 | \n 907 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 908 |     \"\"\"\n 909 |     base_dir = join(dirname(__file__), 'data/')\n 910 |     data_filename = join(base_dir, 'linnerud_exercise.csv')\n 911 |     target_filename = join(base_dir, 'linnerud_physiological.csv')\n 912 | \n 913 |     # Read data\n 914 |     data_exercise = np.loadtxt(data_filename, skiprows=1)\n 915 |     data_physiological = np.loadtxt(target_filename, skiprows=1)\n 916 | \n 917 |     # Read header\n 918 |     with open(data_filename) as f:\n 919 |         header_exercise = f.readline().split()\n 920 |     with open(target_filename) as f:\n 921 |         header_physiological = f.readline().split()\n 922 | \n 923 |     with open(dirname(__file__) + '/descr/linnerud.rst') as f:\n 924 |         descr = f.read()\n 925 | \n 926 |     frame = None\n 927 |     if as_frame:\n 928 |         (frame,\n 929 |          data_exercise,\n 930 |          data_physiological) = _convert_data_dataframe(\"load_linnerud\",\n 931 |                                                        data_exercise,\n 932 |                                                        data_physiological,\n 933 |                                                        header_exercise,\n 934 |                                                        header_physiological)\n 935 |     if return_X_y:\n 936 |         return data_exercise, data_physiological\n 937 | \n 938 |     return Bunch(data=data_exercise,\n 939 |                  feature_names=header_exercise,\n 940 |                  target=data_physiological,\n 941 |                  target_names=header_physiological,\n 942 |                  frame=frame,\n 943 |                  DESCR=descr,\n 944 |                  data_filename=data_filename,\n 945 |                  target_filename=target_filename)\n 946 | \n 947 | \n 948 | @_deprecate_positional_args\n 949 | def load_boston(*, return_X_y=False):\n 950 |     \"\"\"Load and return the boston house-prices dataset (regression).\n 951 | \n 952 |     ==============   ==============\n 953 |     Samples total               506\n 954 |     Dimensionality               13\n 955 |     Features         real, positive\n 956 |     Targets           real 5. - 50.\n 957 |     ==============   ==============\n 958 | \n 959 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <boston_dataset>`.\n 960 | \n 961 |     Parameters\n 962 |     ----------\n 963 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n 964 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object.\n 965 |         See below for more information about the `data` and `target` object.\n 966 | \n 967 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 968 | \n 969 |     Returns\n 970 |     -------\n 971 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 972 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n 973 | \n 974 |         data : ndarray of shape (506, 13)\n 975 |             The data matrix.\n 976 |         target : ndarray of shape (506, )\n 977 |             The regression target.\n 978 |         filename : str\n 979 |             The physical location of boston csv dataset.\n 980 | \n 981 |             .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 982 | \n 983 |         DESCR : str\n 984 |             The full description of the dataset.\n 985 |         feature_names : ndarray\n 986 |             The names of features\n 987 | \n 988 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n 989 | \n 990 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 991 | \n 992 |     Notes\n 993 |     -----\n 994 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.20\n 995 |             Fixed a wrong data point at [445, 0].\n 996 | \n 997 |     Examples\n 998 |     --------\n 999 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import load_boston\n1000 |     >>> X, y = load_boston(return_X_y=True)\n1001 |     >>> print(X.shape)\n1002 |     (506, 13)\n1003 |     \"\"\"\n1004 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n1005 | \n1006 |     fdescr_name = join(module_path, 'descr', 'boston_house_prices.rst')\n1007 |     with open(fdescr_name) as f:\n1008 |         descr_text = f.read()\n1009 | \n1010 |     data_file_name = join(module_path, 'data', 'boston_house_prices.csv')\n1011 |     with open(data_file_name) as f:\n1012 |         data_file = csv.reader(f)\n1013 |         temp = next(data_file)\n1014 |         n_samples = int(temp[0])\n1015 |         n_features = int(temp[1])\n1016 |         data = np.empty((n_samples, n_features))\n1017 |         target = np.empty((n_samples,))\n1018 |         temp = next(data_file)  # names of features\n1019 |         feature_names = np.array(temp)\n1020 | \n1021 |         for i, d in enumerate(data_file):\n1022 |             data[i] = np.asarray(d[:-1], dtype=np.float64)\n1023 |             target[i] = np.asarray(d[-1], dtype=np.float64)\n1024 | \n1025 |     if return_X_y:\n1026 |         return data, target\n1027 | \n1028 |     return Bunch(data=data,\n1029 |                  target=target,\n1030 |                  # last column is target value\n1031 |                  feature_names=feature_names[:-1],\n1032 |                  DESCR=descr_text,\n1033 |                  filename=data_file_name)\n1034 | \n1035 | \n1036 | def load_sample_images():\n1037 |     \"\"\"Load sample images for image manipulation.\n1038 | \n1039 |     Loads both, ``china`` and ``flower``.\n1040 | \n1041 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <sample_images>`.\n1042 | \n1043 |     Returns\n1044 |     -------\n1045 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n1046 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n1047 | \n1048 |         images : list of ndarray of shape (427, 640, 3)\n1049 |             The two sample image.\n1050 |         filenames : list\n1051 |             The filenames for the images.\n1052 |         DESCR : str\n1053 |             The full description of the dataset.\n1054 | \n1055 |     Examples\n1056 |     --------\n1057 |     To load the data and visualize the images:\n1058 | \n1059 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import load_sample_images\n1060 |     >>> dataset = load_sample_images()     #doctest: +SKIP\n1061 |     >>> len(dataset.images)                #doctest: +SKIP\n1062 |     2\n1063 |     >>> first_img_data = dataset.images[0] #doctest: +SKIP\n1064 |     >>> first_img_data.shape               #doctest: +SKIP\n1065 |     (427, 640, 3)\n1066 |     >>> first_img_data.dtype               #doctest: +SKIP\n1067 |     dtype('uint8')\n1068 |     \"\"\"\n1069 |     # import PIL only when needed\n1070 |     from ..externals._pilutil import imread\n1071 | \n1072 |     module_path = join(dirname(__file__), \"images\")\n1073 |     with open(join(module_path, 'README.txt')) as f:\n1074 |         descr = f.read()\n1075 |     filenames = [join(module_path, filename)\n1076 |                  for filename in sorted(os.listdir(module_path))\n1077 |                  if filename.endswith(\".jpg\")]\n1078 |     # Load image data for each image in the source folder.\n1079 |     images = [imread(filename) for filename in filenames]\n1080 | \n1081 |     return Bunch(images=images,\n1082 |                  filenames=filenames,\n1083 |                  DESCR=descr)\n1084 | \n1085 | \n1086 | def load_sample_image(image_name):\n1087 |     \"\"\"Load the numpy array of a single sample image\n1088 | \n1089 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <sample_images>`.\n1090 | \n1091 |     Parameters\n1092 |     ----------\n1093 |     image_name : {`china.jpg`, `flower.jpg`}\n1094 |         The name of the sample image loaded\n1095 | \n1096 |     Returns\n1097 |     -------\n1098 |     img : 3D array\n1099 |         The image as a numpy array: height x width x color\n1100 | \n1101 |     Examples\n1102 |     --------\n1103 | \n1104 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import load_sample_image\n1105 |     >>> china = load_sample_image('china.jpg')   # doctest: +SKIP\n1106 |     >>> china.dtype                              # doctest: +SKIP\n1107 |     dtype('uint8')\n1108 |     >>> china.shape                              # doctest: +SKIP\n1109 |     (427, 640, 3)\n1110 |     >>> flower = load_sample_image('flower.jpg') # doctest: +SKIP\n1111 |     >>> flower.dtype                             # doctest: +SKIP\n1112 |     dtype('uint8')\n1113 |     >>> flower.shape                             # doctest: +SKIP\n1114 |     (427, 640, 3)\n1115 |     \"\"\"\n1116 |     images = load_sample_images()\n1117 |     index = None\n1118 |     for i, filename in enumerate(images.filenames):\n1119 |         if filename.endswith(image_name):\n1120 |             index = i\n1121 |             break\n1122 |     if index is None:\n1123 |         raise AttributeError(\"Cannot find sample image: %s\" % image_name)\n1124 |     return images.images[index]\n1125 | \n1126 | \n1127 | def _pkl_filepath(*args, **kwargs):\n1128 |     \"\"\"Return filename for Python 3 pickles\n1129 | \n1130 |     args[-1] is expected to be the \".pkl\" filename. For compatibility with\n1131 |     older scikit-learn versions, a suffix is inserted before the extension.\n1132 | \n1133 |     _pkl_filepath('/path/to/folder', 'filename.pkl') returns\n1134 |     '/path/to/folder/filename_py3.pkl'\n1135 | \n1136 |     \"\"\"\n1137 |     py3_suffix = kwargs.get(\"py3_suffix\", \"_py3\")\n1138 |     basename, ext = splitext(args[-1])\n1139 |     basename += py3_suffix\n1140 |     new_args = args[:-1] + (basename + ext,)\n1141 |     return join(*new_args)\n1142 | \n1143 | \n1144 | def _sha256(path):\n1145 |     \"\"\"Calculate the sha256 hash of the file at path.\"\"\"\n1146 |     sha256hash = hashlib.sha256()\n1147 |     chunk_size = 8192\n1148 |     with open(path, \"rb\") as f:\n1149 |         while True:\n1150 |             buffer = f.read(chunk_size)\n1151 |             if not buffer:\n1152 |                 break\n1153 |             sha256hash.update(buffer)\n1154 |     return sha256hash.hexdigest()\n1155 | \n1156 | \n1157 | def _fetch_remote(remote, dirname=None):\n1158 |     \"\"\"Helper function to download a remote dataset into path\n1159 | \n1160 |     Fetch a dataset pointed by remote's url, save into path using remote's\n1161 |     filename and ensure its integrity based on the SHA256 Checksum of the\n1162 |     downloaded file.\n1163 | \n1164 |     Parameters\n1165 |     ----------\n1166 |     remote : RemoteFileMetadata\n1167 |         Named tuple containing remote dataset meta information: url, filename\n1168 |         and checksum\n1169 | \n1170 |     dirname : string\n1171 |         Directory to save the file to.\n1172 | \n1173 |     Returns\n1174 |     -------\n1175 |     file_path: string\n1176 |         Full path of the created file.\n1177 |     \"\"\"\n1178 | \n1179 |     file_path = (remote.filename if dirname is None\n1180 |                  else join(dirname, remote.filename))\n1181 |     urlretrieve(remote.url, file_path)\n1182 |     checksum = _sha256(file_path)\n1183 |     if remote.checksum != checksum:\n1184 |         raise IOError(\"{} has an SHA256 checksum ({}) \"\n1185 |                       \"differing from expected ({}), \"\n1186 |                       \"file may be corrupted.\".format(file_path, checksum,\n1187 |                                                       remote.checksum))\n1188 |     return file_path\n1189 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me analyze the code and locate the relevant areas for implementing MD5 checksum validation. Based on the issue description and code analysis, I'll need to modify the following files:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 488-604\nfunction: fetch_openml\n# This is where we need to add MD5 checksum validation logic\n\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py\nline: 1144-1154\nfunction: _sha256\n# Using this helper function for MD5 checksum calculation\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 508-510\nfunction: _stream_checksum_generator\n# Reference implementation of checksum generation\n\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py\nline: 1179-1188\nfunction: _fetch_remote\n# Shows how checksum validation is done with SHA256\n\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py\nline: 26\n# Defines RemoteFileMetadata class used for storing metadata including checksum\n```\n\nThe core modifications needed are:\n\n1. In `fetch_openml()` function, we need to:\n   - Retrieve the MD5 checksum from the dataset description\n   - Calculate the actual MD5 checksum of the downloaded data\n   - Compare them and raise an error if they don't match\n\n2. The MD5 checksum validation logic should be optional, controlled by a parameter (similar to how cache handling is optional).\n\n3. We can use the existing `_sha256()` function from `_base.py` as a reference for implementing MD5 checksum validation.\n\n4. The `_stream_checksum_generator()` function shows how to properly handle streaming large files while calculating checksums.\n\n5. The `RemoteFileMetadata` class in `_base.py` provides the structure needed to store and manage checksum information.\n\nThese locations form the complete picture of where the MD5 checksum validation should be implemented to address the issue.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 89485, "completion_tokens": 558}, "time": 239.2860860824585}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-59900", "found_files": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py", "pandas/core/dtypes/common.py", "pandas/core/dtypes/inference.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods", "pandas/core/dtypes/common.py:is_dtype_equal", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/converter.py:PandasAutoDateLocator", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:MultiColumn", "asv_bench/benchmarks/arithmetic.py:DateInferOps", "asv_bench/benchmarks/multiindex_object.py:Isin", "asv_bench/benchmarks/series_methods.py:ToNumpy", "pandas/io/json/_json.py:JSONTableWriter", "asv_bench/benchmarks/io/csv.py:ReadCSVEngine", "pandas/core/arrays/masked.py:BaseMaskedArray", "pandas/core/dtypes/inference.py:is_hashable"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods._validate", "pandas/core/dtypes/common.py:is_dtype_equal", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/converter.py:PandasAutoDateLocator._get_unit", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:MultiColumn.time_cython_sum", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:MultiColumn.time_col_select_lambda_sum", "asv_bench/benchmarks/arithmetic.py:DateInferOps.time_subtract_datetimes", "asv_bench/benchmarks/multiindex_object.py:Isin.time_isin_large", "asv_bench/benchmarks/arithmetic.py:DateInferOps.time_timedelta_plus_datetime", "asv_bench/benchmarks/series_methods.py:ToNumpy.time_to_numpy_float_with_nan", "pandas/io/json/_json.py:JSONTableWriter.obj_to_write", "asv_bench/benchmarks/io/csv.py:ReadCSVEngine.time_read_bytescsv", "pandas/core/arrays/masked.py:BaseMaskedArray.__abs__", "pandas/core/dtypes/inference.py:is_hashable"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on my analysis, I can now provide the locations that need modification to implement the requested feature:\n\n```\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 257-258\nfunction: StringMethods._validate\n\npandas/core/dtypes/common.py\nline: 38-39\nfunction: is_dtype_equal\n\npandas/core/dtypes/_common.py\nline: 1776\nfunction: extract_array\n\npandas/core/dtypes/inference.py\nline: 373-424\nfunction: is_hashable\n\npandas/_libs/lib.py\nline: 387-388\nfunction: infer_dtype\n```\n\nExplanation of why these locations are relevant:\n\n1. `pandas/core/strings/accessor.py` is the main file needing modification as it contains the specific error message that needs to be improved. The key line is 258 where the AttributeError is raised without showing the inferred dtype.\n\n2. `pandas/core/dtypes/common.py` contains utility functions for type checking and conversion that are used in the validation process.\n\n3. `pandas/core/dtypes/_common.py` contains the `extract_array` function that prepares the data for type checking.\n\n4. `pandas/core/dtypes/inference.py` contains the type checking logic that helps determine the data type.\n\n5. `pandas/_libs/lib.py` contains the core C++ implementation of type inference that powers the `infer_dtype` function.\n\nThe core change would be in `pandas/core/strings/accessor.py` at line 258, where the error message string needs to be updated to include the inferred dtype. The other files provide the supporting infrastructure that makes this feature work properly."], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "8d2ca0bf84bcf44a800ac19bdb4ed7ec88c555e2", "problem_statement": "ENH: increase verbosity on error\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nindex 3cb0e75cfb815..05e1a36877e06 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n@@ -255,7 +255,9 @@ def _validate(data):\n         inferred_dtype = lib.infer_dtype(values, skipna=True)\n \n         if inferred_dtype not in allowed_types:\n-            raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")\n+            raise AttributeError(\n+                f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values, not {inferred_dtype}\"\n+            )\n         return inferred_dtype\n \n     def __getitem__(self, key):\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 85786, "completion_tokens": 578}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'pandas-dev' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: ENH: increase verbosity on error\n\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental context information:\n\nYou will receive supplemental context for the current task. Use it to better understand the issue and to localize the relevant code more accurately.\n\nTreat this context as additional evidence, not as the final answer.\n- It may contain noise.\n- It may be incomplete.\n- You should carefully analyze it and decide how to use it when searching for relevant code locations.\n\nDepending on the settings, the supplemental context may include:\n1. Unique artifacts\n2. Unique dependency tree\n\n## How to use this supplemental context\n- Use it to identify candidate files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue.\n- Cross-check it against the issue description and the repository evidence you collect.\n- Do not assume every artifact is important.\n- Do not assume the provided context is sufficient by itself.\n\n## What the provided information means\nAssume the issue sent to you is a feature request requesting feature F.\n\n1. Unique artifacts\n- The unique artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis.\n- For the requested feature described in the issue text, two Python scripts, A.py and B.py, describing the same process are executed and traced.\n- The only difference is that A.py is executed with feature F enabled, while B.py is executed with the feature disabled.\n- By comparing the two tracing results, the overlapping part between the trace for A.py and B.py is subtracted.\n- The remaining artifacts are the unique artifacts that appear only in A.py.\n- These unique artifacts are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\n2. Unique dependency tree\n- The unique dependency tree is obtained by further analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts.\n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts.\n- It can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\n\nHere is the information about the current task:\n1. Unique artifacts (obtained through Software Reconnaissance-style analysis):\n# Unique Artifacts\n\nUse these unique artifacts as supplemental hints, not as final answers.\n\n## Summary\n- Trace A role: `feature_positive`\n- Trace B role: `feature_negative`\n- Comparison: `trace_a_minus_trace_b`\n- Unique functions in JSON: `44`\n- Unique functions shown here: `42`\n- Target package filter: `pandas`\n\n## Related Files\n- `pandas/core/construction.py`: `7` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/internals/managers.py`: `6` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/generic.py`: `4` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/series.py`: `4` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/indexes/base.py`: `3` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/internals/blocks.py`: `3` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/dtypes/cast.py`: `2` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/dtypes/common.py`: `2` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/dtypes/generic.py`: `2` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/indexes/range.py`: `2` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/strings/accessor.py`: `2` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/accessor.py`: `1` unique function(s)\n\n## Artifact Candidates\n- artifact: `pandas/core/series.py:Series.__init__`\n  - module: `pandas.core.series`\n  - definition: `lines 364-510`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 372`\n  - observed lines: `372, 373, 374, 394, 398, 399, 401, 413, ...`\n  - trace activity: 32 events, 1 calls, 30 line events, 1 returns