et_config`.\n37 | \n38 |     Returns\n39 |     -------\n40 |     config : dict\n41 |         Keys are parameter names that can be passed to :func:`set_config`.\n42 | \n43 |     See Also\n44 |     --------\n45 |     config_context : Context manager for global scikit-learn configuration.\n46 |     set_config : Set global scikit-learn configuration.\n47 | \n48 |     Examples\n49 |     --------\n50 |     >>> import sklearn\n51 |     >>> config = sklearn.get_config()\n52 |     >>> config.keys()\n53 |     dict_keys([...])\n54 |     \"\"\"\n55 |     # Return a copy of the threadlocal configuration so that users will\n56 |     # not be able to modify the configuration with the returned dict.\n57 |     return _get_threadlocal_config().copy()\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on my analysis of the code and the issue description, I can now provide the locations that need to be modified to implement the requested feature. Here's the localized code:\n\n```\nsklearn/metrics/_regression.py\nline: 58-137\nfunction: _check_reg_targets\n\nsklearn/metrics/_regression.py\nline: 204-211\nfunction: mean_absolute_error\n\nsklearn/metrics/_regression.py\nline: 305-307\nfunction: mean_absolute_percentage_error\n\nsklearn/metrics/_regression.py\nline: 496-501\nfunction: mean_squared_error\n\nsklearn/metrics/_regression.py\nline: 672-677\nfunction: root_mean_squared_log_error\n```\n\nExplanation of why these locations are relevant:\n\n1. `_check_reg_targets` is the core function that needs to be modified as it currently receives `dtype` as a separate parameter but returns it back to be used in `_find_matching_floating_dtype`.\n\n2. The other regression metrics (mean_absolute_error, mean_absolute_percentage_error, mean_squared_error, root_mean_squared_error) all follow the same pattern where they:\n   - First call `_check_reg_targets` with the dtype parameter\n   - Then pass through another floating-point dtype calculation via `_find_matching_floating_dtype`\n   - Finally call `_check_reg_targets` again with the returned dtype\n\n3. The issue suggests creating a new function `_check_reg_targets_and_dtype` that would combine these operations, but based on the current implementation patterns, these modifications are sufficient to eliminate redundant floating-point type checks throughout the regression metrics.\n\n4. The implementation should focus on these specific locations because:\n   - They're all part of the Array API project (#26024)\n   - They're all in the `_regression.py` file\n   - They all follow the same pattern of floating-point type handling\n   - They're all decorated with `@validate_params` which would need to be maintained\n\nThese locations form the complete set of code that would need to be updated to implement the suggested changes to reduce redundancy in floating type checks across most regression metrics.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "Verify if the found locations contain all the necessary information to address the issue, and check for any relevant references in other parts of the codebase that may not have appeared in the search results. If not, continue searching for additional locations related to the issue.\nVerify that you have carefully analyzed the impact of the found locations on the repository, especially their dependencies. If you think you have solved the task, rewrite your final answer into the required location-only format, output ONLY the location block, and then call `finish` to finish.\nIMPORTANT: YOU SHOULD NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\n"}, {"content": "Let me verify the completeness of the found locations and check for additional related code:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_find_matching_floating_dtype', 'dtype', 'get_namespace']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:check_array\nbenchmarks/bench_saga.py:plot\nsklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_transformer_preserve_dtypes\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_sample_weight\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_BaseNMF._check_params\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_BaseNMF._check_w_h\nsklearn/impute/_base.py:SimpleImputer._validate_input\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:check_array\nsklearn/externals/_scipy/sparse/csgraph/_laplacian.py:_laplacian_dense\nsklearn/metrics/_pairwise_distances_reduction/_dispatcher.py:ArgKminClassMode.compute\n##Searching for term \"_find_matching_floating_dtype\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_find_matching_floating_dtype`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_find_matching_floating_dtype`.\n```\n673 | def _find_matching_floating_dtype(*arrays, xp):\n674 |     \"\"\"Find a suitable floating point dtype when computing with arrays.\n675 | \n676 |     If any of the arrays are floating point, return the dtype with the highest\n677 |     precision by following official type promotion rules:\n678 | \n679 |     https://data-apis.org/array-api/latest/API_specification/type_promotion.html\n680 | \n681 |     If there are no floating point input arrays (all integral inputs for\n682 |     instance), return the default floating point dtype for the namespace.\n683 |     \"\"\"\n684 |     dtyped_arrays = [a for a in arrays if hasattr(a, \"dtype\")]\n685 |     floating_dtypes = [\n686 |         a.dtype for a in dtyped_arrays if xp.isdtype(a.dtype, \"real floating\")\n687 |     ]\n688 |     if floating_dtypes:\n689 |         # Return the floating dtype with the highest precision:\n690 |         return xp.result_type(*floating_dtypes)\n691 | \n692 |     # If none of the input arrays have a floating point dtype, they must be all\n693 |     # integer arrays or containers of Python scalars: return the default\n694 |     # floating point dtype for the namespace (implementation specific).\n695 |     return xp.asarray(0.0).dtype\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"dtype\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound `sklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:_return_float_dtype`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  50 | # Utility Functions\n  51 | def _return_float_dtype(X, Y):\n  52 |     \"\"\"\n  53 |     1. If dtype of X and Y is float32, then dtype float32 is returned.\n  54 |     2. Else dtype float is returned.\n  55 |     \"\"\"\n  56 |     if not issparse(X) and not isinstance(X, np.ndarray):\n  57 |         X = np.asarray(X)\n  58 | \n  59 |     if Y is None:\n  60 |         Y_dtype = X.dtype\n  61 |     elif not issparse(Y) and not isinstance(Y, np.ndarray):\n  62 |         Y = np.asarray(Y)\n  63 |         Y_dtype = Y.dtype\n  64 |     else:\n  65 |         Y_dtype = Y.dtype\n  66 | \n  67 |     if X.dtype == Y_dtype == np.float32:\n  68 |         dtype = np.float32\n  69 |     else:\n  70 |         dtype = float\n  71 | \n  72 |     return X, Y, dtype\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_array`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 735 | def check_array(\n 736 |     array,\n 737 |     accept_sparse=False,\n 738 |     *,\n 739 |     accept_large_sparse=True,\n 740 |     dtype=\"numeric\",\n 741 |     order=None,\n 742 |     copy=False,\n 743 |     force_writeable=False,\n 744 |     force_all_finite=\"deprecated\",\n 745 |     ensure_all_finite=None,\n 746 |     ensure_non_negative=False,\n 747 |     ensure_2d=True,\n 748 |     allow_nd=False,\n 749 |     ensure_min_samples=1,\n 750 |     ensure_min_features=1,\n 751 |     estimator=None,\n 752 |     input_name=\"\",\n 753 | ):\n 754 |     \"\"\"Input validation on an array, list, sparse matrix or similar.\n 755 | \n 756 |     By default, the input is checked to be a non-empty 2D array containing\n 757 |     only finite values. If the dtype of the array is object, attempt\n 758 |     converting to float, raising on failure.\n 759 | \n 760 |     Parameters\n 761 |     ----------\n 762 |     array : object\n 763 |         Input object to check / convert.\n 764 | \n 765 |     accept_sparse : str, bool or list/tuple of str, default=False\n 766 |         String[s] representing allowed sparse matrix formats, such as 'csc',\n 767 |         'csr', etc. If the input is sparse but not in the allowed format,\n 768 |         it will be converted to the first listed format. True allows the input\n 769 |         to be any format. False means that a sparse matrix input will\n 770 |         raise an error.\n 771 | \n 772 |     accept_large_sparse : bool, default=True\n 773 |         If a CSR, CSC, COO or BSR sparse matrix is supplied and accepted by\n 774 |         accept_sparse, accept_large_sparse=False will cause it to be accepted\n 775 |         only if its indices are stored with a 32-bit dtype.\n 776 | \n 777 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 778 | \n 779 |     dtype : 'numeric', type, list of type or None, default='numeric'\n 780 |         Data type of result. If None, the dtype of the input is preserved.\n 781 |         If \"numeric\", dtype is preserved unless array.dtype is object.\n 782 |         If dtype is a list of types, conversion on the first type is only\n 783 |         performed if the dtype of the input is not in the list.\n 784 | \n 785 |     order : {'F', 'C'} or None, default=None\n 786 |         Whether an array will be forced to be fortran or c-style.\n 787 |         When order is None (default), then if copy=False, nothing is ensured\n 788 |         about the memory layout of the output array; otherwise (copy=True)\n 789 |         the memory layout of the returned array is kept as close as possible\n 790 |         to the original array.\n 791 | \n 792 |     copy : bool, default=False\n 793 |         Whether a forced copy will be triggered. If copy=False, a copy might\n 794 |         be triggered by a conversion.\n 795 | \n 796 |     force_writeable : bool, default=False\n 797 |         Whether to force the output array to be writeable. If True, the returned array\n 798 |         is guaranteed to be writeable, which may require a copy. Otherwise the\n 799 |         writeability of the input array is preserved.\n 800 | \n 801 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n 802 | \n 803 |     force_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan', default=True\n 804 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array. The\n 805 |         possibilities are:\n 806 | \n 807 |         - True: Force all values of array to be finite.\n 808 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array.\n 809 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan and pd.NA values in array. Values\n 810 |           cannot be infinite.\n 811 | \n 812 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 813 |            ``force_all_finite`` accepts the string ``'allow-nan'``.\n 814 | \n 815 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 816 |            Accepts `pd.NA` and converts it into `np.nan`\n 817 | \n 818 |         .. deprecated:: 1.6\n 819 |            `force_all_finite` was renamed to `ensure_all_finite` and will be removed\n 820 |            in 1.8.\n 821 | \n 822 |     ensure_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan', default=True\n 823 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array. The\n 824 |         possibilities are:\n 825 | \n 826 |         - True: Force all values of array to be finite.\n 827 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array.\n 828 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan and pd.NA values in array. Values\n 829 |           cannot be infinite.\n 830 | \n 831 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n 832 |            `force_all_finite` was renamed to `ensure_all_finite`.\n 833 | \n 834 |     ensure_non_negative : bool, default=False\n 835 |         Make sure the array has only non-negative values. If True, an array that\n 836 |         contains negative values will raise a ValueError.\n 837 | \n 838 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n 839 | \n 840 |     ensure_2d : bool, default=True\n 841 |         Whether to raise a value error if array is not 2D.\n 842 | \n 843 |     allow_nd : bool, default=False\n 844 |         Whether to allow array.ndim > 2.\n 845 | \n 846 |     ensure_min_samples : int, default=1\n 847 |         Make sure that the array has a minimum number of samples in its first\n 848 |         axis (rows for a 2D array). Setting to 0 disables this check.\n 849 | \n 850 |     ensure_min_features : int, default=1\n 851 |         Make sure that the 2D array has some minimum number of features\n 852 |         (columns). The default value of 1 rejects empty datasets.\n 853 |         This check is only enforced when the input data has effectively 2\n 854 |         dimensions or is originally 1D and ``ensure_2d`` is True. Setting to 0\n 855 |         disables this check.\n 856 | \n 857 |     estimator : str or estimator instance, default=None\n 858 |         If passed, include the name of the estimator in warning messages.\n 859 | \n 860 |     input_name : str, default=\"\"\n 861 |         The data name used to construct the error message. In particular\n 862 |         if `input_name` is \"X\" and the data has NaN values and\n 863 |         allow_nan is False, the error message will link to the imputer\n 864 |         documentation.\n 865 | \n 866 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1.0\n 867 | \n 868 |     Returns\n 869 |     -------\n 870 |     array_converted : object\n 871 |         The converted and validated array.\n 872 | \n 873 |     Examples\n 874 |     --------\n 875 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_array\n 876 |     >>> X = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\n 877 |     >>> X_checked = check_array(X)\n 878 |     >>> X_checked\n 879 |     array([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])\n 880 |     \"\"\"\n 881 |     ensure_all_finite = _deprecate_force_all_finite(force_all_finite, ensure_all_finite)\n 882 | \n 883 |     if isinstance(array, np.matrix):\n 884 |         raise TypeError(\n 885 |             \"np.matrix is not supported. Please convert to a numpy array with \"\n 886 |             \"np.asarray. For more information see: \"\n 887 |             \"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.matrix.html\"\n 888 |         )\n 889 | \n 890 |     xp, is_array_api_compliant = get_namespace(array)\n 891 | \n 892 |     # store reference to original array to check if copy is needed when\n 893 |     # function returns\n 894 |     array_orig = array\n 895 | \n 896 |     # store whether originally we wanted numeric dtype\n 897 |     dtype_numeric = isinstance(dtype, str) and dtype == \"numeric\"\n 898 | \n 899 |     dtype_orig = getattr(array, \"dtype\", None)\n 900 |     if not is_array_api_compliant and not hasattr(dtype_orig, \"kind\"):\n 901 |         # not a data type (e.g. a column named dtype in a pandas DataFrame)\n 902 |         dtype_orig = None\n 903 | \n 904 |     # check if the object contains several dtypes (typically a pandas\n 905 |     # DataFrame), and store them. If not, store None.\n 906 |     dtypes_orig = None\n 907 |     pandas_requires_conversion = False\n 908 |     # track if we have a Series-like object to raise a better error message\n 909 |     type_if_series = None\n 910 |     if hasattr(array, \"dtypes\") and hasattr(array.dtypes, \"__array__\"):\n 911 |         # throw warning if columns are sparse. If all columns are sparse, then\n 912 |         # array.sparse exists and sparsity will be preserved (later).\n 913 |         with suppress(ImportError):\n 914 |             from pandas import SparseDtype\n 915 | \n 916 |             def is_sparse(dtype):\n 917 |                 return isinstance(dtype, SparseDtype)\n 918 | \n 919 |             if not hasattr(array, \"sparse\") and array.dtypes.apply(is_sparse).any():\n 920 |                 warnings.warn(\n 921 |                     \"pandas.DataFrame with sparse columns found.\"\n 922 |                     \"It will be converted to a dense numpy array.\"\n 923 |                 )\n 924 | \n 925 |         dtypes_orig = list(array.dtypes)\n 926 |         pandas_requires_conversion = any(\n 927 |             _pandas_dtype_needs_early_conversion(i) for i in dtypes_orig\n 928 |         )\n 929 |         if all(isinstance(dtype_iter, np.dtype) for dtype_iter in dtypes_orig):\n 930 |             dtype_orig = np.result_type(*dtypes_orig)\n 931 |         elif pandas_requires_conversion and any(d == object for d in dtypes_orig):\n 932 |             # Force object if any of the dtypes is an object\n 933 |             dtype_orig = object\n 934 | \n 935 |     elif (_is_extension_array_dtype(array) or hasattr(array, \"iloc\")) and hasattr(\n 936 |         array, \"dtype\"\n 937 |     ):\n 938 |         # array is a pandas series\n 939 |         type_if_series = type(array)\n 940 |         pandas_requires_conversion = _pandas_dtype_needs_early_conversion(array.dtype)\n 941 |         if isinstance(array.dtype, np.dtype):\n 942 |             dtype_orig = array.dtype\n 943 |         else:\n 944 |             # Set to None to let array.astype work out the best dtype\n 945 |             dtype_orig = None\n 946 | \n 947 |     if dtype_numeric:\n 948 |         if (\n 949 |             dtype_orig is not None\n 950 |             and hasattr(dtype_orig, \"kind\")\n 951 |             and dtype_orig.kind == \"O\"\n 952 |         ):\n 953 |             # if input is object, convert to float.\n 954 |             dtype = xp.float64\n 955 |         else:\n 956 |             dtype = None\n 957 | \n 958 |     if isinstance(dtype, (list, tuple)):\n 959 |         if dtype_orig is not None and dtype_orig in dtype:\n 960 |             # no dtype conversion required\n 961 |             dtype = None\n 962 |         else:\n 963 |             # dtype conversion required. Let's select the first element of the\n 964 |             # list of accepted types.\n 965 |             dtype = dtype[0]\n 966 | \n 967 |     if pandas_requires_conversion:\n 968 |         # pandas dataframe requires conversion earlier to handle extension dtypes with\n 969 |         # nans\n 970 |         # Use the original dtype for conversion if dtype is None\n 971 |         new_dtype = dtype_orig if dtype is None else dtype\n 972 |         array = array.astype(new_dtype)\n 973 |         # Since we converted here, we do not need to convert again later\n 974 |         dtype = None\n 975 | \n 976 |     if ensure_all_finite not in (True, False, \"allow-nan\"):\n 977 |         raise ValueError(\n 978 |             \"ensure_all_finite should be a bool or 'allow-nan'. Got \"\n 979 |             f\"{ensure_all_finite!r} instead.\"\n 980 |         )\n 981 | \n 982 |     if dtype is not None and _is_numpy_namespace(xp):\n 983 |         # convert to dtype object to conform to Array API to be use `xp.isdtype` later\n 984 |         dtype = np.dtype(dtype)\n 985 | \n 986 |     estimator_name = _check_estimator_name(estimator)\n 987 |     context = \" by %s\" % estimator_name if estimator is not None else \"\"\n 988 | \n 989 |     # When all dataframe columns are sparse, convert to a sparse array\n 990 |     if hasattr(array, \"sparse\") and array.ndim > 1:\n 991 |         with suppress(ImportError):\n 992 |             from pandas import SparseDtype  # noqa: F811\n 993 | \n 994 |             def is_sparse(dtype):\n 995 |                 return isinstance(dtype, SparseDtype)\n 996 | \n 997 |             if array.dtypes.apply(is_sparse).all():\n 998 |                 # DataFrame.sparse only supports `to_coo`\n 999 |                 array = array.sparse.to_coo()\n1000 |                 if array.dtype == np.dtype(\"object\"):\n1001 |                     unique_dtypes = set([dt.subtype.name for dt in array_orig.dtypes])\n1002 |                     if len(unique_dtypes) > 1:\n1003 |                         raise ValueError(\n1004 |                             \"Pandas DataFrame with mixed sparse extension arrays \"\n1005 |                             \"generated a sparse matrix with object dtype which \"\n1006 |                             \"can not be co