\n3648 |             sort direction can be controlled for each level individually.\n3649 |         inplace : bool, default False\n3650 |             If True, perform operation in-place.\n3651 |         kind : {'quicksort', 'mergesort', 'heapsort', 'stable'}, default 'quicksort'\n3652 |             Choice of sorting algorithm. See also :func:`numpy.sort` for more\n3653 |             information. 'mergesort' and 'stable' are the only stable algorithms. For\n3654 |             DataFrames, this option is only applied when sorting on a single\n3655 |             column or label.\n3656 |         na_position : {'first', 'last'}, default 'last'\n3657 |             If 'first' puts NaNs at the beginning, 'last' puts NaNs at the end.\n3658 |             Not implemented for MultiIndex.\n3659 |         sort_remaining : bool, default True\n3660 |             If True and sorting by level and index is multilevel, sort by other\n3661 |             levels too (in order) after sorting by specified level.\n3662 |         ignore_index : bool, default False\n3663 |             If True, the resulting axis will be labeled 0, 1, \u2026, n - 1.\n3664 |         key : callable, optional\n3665 |             If not None, apply the key function to the index values\n3666 |             before sorting. This is similar to the `key` argument in the\n3667 |             builtin :meth:`sorted` function, with the notable difference that\n3668 |             this `key` function should be *vectorized*. It should expect an\n3669 |             ``Index`` and return an ``Index`` of the same shape.\n3670 | \n3671 |         Returns\n3672 |         -------\n3673 |         Series or None\n3674 |             The original Series sorted by the labels or None if ``inplace=True``.\n3675 | \n3676 |         See Also\n3677 |         --------\n3678 |         DataFrame.sort_index: Sort DataFrame by the index.\n3679 |         DataFrame.sort_values: Sort DataFrame by the value.\n3680 |         Series.sort_values : Sort Series by the value.\n3681 | \n3682 |         Examples\n3683 |         --------\n3684 |         >>> s = pd.Series([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\"], index=[3, 2, 1, 4])\n3685 |         >>> s.sort_index()\n3686 |         1    c\n3687 |         2    b\n3688 |         3    a\n3689 |         4    d\n3690 |         dtype: object\n3691 | \n3692 |         Sort Descending\n3693 | \n3694 |         >>> s.sort_index(ascending=False)\n3695 |         4    d\n3696 |         3    a\n3697 |         2    b\n3698 |         1    c\n3699 |         dtype: object\n3700 | \n3701 |         By default NaNs are put at the end, but use `na_position` to place\n3702 |         them at the beginning\n3703 | \n3704 |         >>> s = pd.Series([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\"], index=[3, 2, 1, np.nan])\n3705 |         >>> s.sort_index(na_position=\"first\")\n3706 |         NaN     d\n3707 |          1.0    c\n3708 |          2.0    b\n3709 |          3.0    a\n3710 |         dtype: object\n3711 | \n3712 |         Specify index level to sort\n3713 | \n3714 |         >>> arrays = [\n3715 |         ...     np.array([\"qux\", \"qux\", \"foo\", \"foo\", \"baz\", \"baz\", \"bar\", \"bar\"]),\n3716 |         ...     np.array([\"two\", \"one\", \"two\", \"one\", \"two\", \"one\", \"two\", \"one\"]),\n3717 |         ... ]\n3718 |         >>> s = pd.Series([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8], index=arrays)\n3719 |         >>> s.sort_index(level=1)\n3720 |         bar  one    8\n3721 |         baz  one    6\n3722 |         foo  one    4\n3723 |         qux  one    2\n3724 |         bar  two    7\n3725 |         baz  two    5\n3726 |         foo  two    3\n3727 |         qux  two    1\n3728 |         dtype: int64\n3729 | \n3730 |         Does not sort by remaining levels when sorting by levels\n3731 | \n3732 |         >>> s.sort_index(level=1, sort_remaining=False)\n3733 |         qux  one    2\n3734 |         foo  one    4\n3735 |         baz  one    6\n3736 |         bar  one    8\n3737 |         qux  two    1\n3738 |         foo  two  